{"ts": 1789770908.005219, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Signs are unambiguous \u2014 all-positive dominates, confirming each feature contributes real directional signal rather than a sign fit to noise.\n\nNow let me calibrate my OOS forecast honestly rather than just quoting the in-sample number.\n\n**Reasoning about shrinkage:**\n\n- In-sample Sharpe: **1.233** over n=5000 days.\n- I ran ~45 evaluations and selected the maximum. With 40 features \u00d7 signs \u00d7 up-to-3 combos, the effective search space is large (~10\u2074 specs), so the selected maximum is upward-biased. The tool's own search-adjusted bar (1.023) quantifies this: it's roughly the Sharpe a *lucky* search would produce on noise.\n- My edge over the bar is only ~0.21, which is the defensible part of the result.\n- Standard error of a Sharpe at n=5000 (~20yr daily) is \u2248 \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 annualized \u2014 so even the in-sample estimate has real sampling noise.\n- Mitigating factor: feature 24 alone cleared the bar *before* any combinatorial search, on its first look. That's a pre-search finding and much less contaminated by selection. The triple's increment over 24 alone (1.059\u21921.233) is the part most likely to shrink.\n\nI'll center my belief near feature 24's standalone level, crediting only partial persistence of the combination gain."}
