{"ts": 1789770958.499747, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Result\n\n**Submitted:** features `[24, 9, 8]`, all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.233** (n=5000).\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.85 \u00b1 0.35**\n\n### How I got there\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually** (80 implicit sign tests). Feature **24** was a clear outlier at 1.059; everything else fell in roughly \u00b10.6, consistent with noise plus a few weak signals.\n2. **Paired 24 with the strongest others.** Gains were modest, confirming 24 carries most of the signal.\n3. **Tested triples.** `[24, 9, 8]` reached 1.233.\n4. **Verified the mechanism.** Features 9 and 8 are individually positive (0.577, 0.507) and their pair (0.766) exceeds either alone \u2014 so the triple's gain comes from genuine imperfect correlation between real signals, not from stacking noise. Sign flips degraded performance sharply (1.233 \u2192 0.65 / 0.57 / 0.02), so the directions are firmly identified rather than fit to noise.\n\n### Why I forecast well below the in-sample number\n\nI deliberately did **not** predict ~1.23. Three considerations drove the shrinkage to 0.85:\n\n- **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~45 specs and reported the maximum. The search-adjusted bar of 1.023 is effectively the tool's estimate of what a lucky search yields on pure noise \u2014 my genuine edge over it is only ~0.21.\n- **Sampling error.** At n=5000, a Sharpe estimate carries roughly \u00b10.22 annualized standard error even without any selection.\n- **What's most credible.** Feature 24 cleared the bar on the *very first pass*, before any combinatorial search \u2014 that's a pre-search finding and largely uncontaminated. The increment from 1.059 to 1.233 came *after* searching, so it's the portion most likely to evaporate. I centered near 24's standalone level and credited only partial persistence of the combination gain.\n\nThe sd of 0.35 is wide enough to cover a meaningful chance the whole effect is weaker than it appears, while still expressing genuine confidence that this specification has real predictive content.\n\n### One housekeeping note\n\nAt the start of the session, a large number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Google Drive, Github, Linear, and ~30 others) reported that they require OAuth authorization. This session is non-interactive, so I could not run the auth flow. **Those connectors are unavailable until you authorize them** \u2014 for claude.ai connectors via your claude.ai connector settings, or for other servers via `claude mcp` or `/mcp` in an interactive session. None of them were needed for this task, which used only `evaluate`, `status`, and `submit`, so the analysis was unaffected."}
